from author import Bank,save_batch
banks=[]
b=Bank('subject-03','devices','semiconductor-physics','Electronic Devices · Transport, Junctions & Transistors','004');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'In a nondegenerate semiconductor at thermal equilibrium, n0=10¹⁶ cm⁻³ and ni=10¹⁰ cm⁻³. Find p0 in cm⁻³.',10000,'n₀=10¹⁶ cm⁻³; nᵢ=10¹⁰ cm⁻³; thermal equilibrium','Find minority-hole concentration.','Mass-action law',r'n_0p_0=n_i^2',r'Square the intrinsic concentration: ni²=10²⁰ cm⁻⁶. | Divide by n₀=10¹⁶ cm⁻³. | The hole concentration is p₀=10⁴ cm⁻³.','Mass action applies at equilibrium; it need not hold for excess carriers under injection.',difficulty='easy',unit='cm⁻³')
b.n(r'Ignoring holes, a semiconductor has n=10¹⁵ cm⁻³ and electron mobility 1000 cm²/(V·s). Using q=1.6×10⁻¹⁹ C, find conductivity in S/cm.',0.16,'n=10¹⁵ cm⁻³; μn=1000 cm²/(V·s); hole conduction neglected','Find electron conductivity.','Drift conductivity',r'\sigma=q n\mu_n',r'Electron drift current density is qnμnE, so conductivity is its coefficient of E. | Multiply 1.6×10⁻¹⁹×10¹⁵×1000. | The result is 0.16 S/cm; the units follow the centimeter-based input data.','Do not accidentally report S/m without converting the centimeter units.',topic='carrier-transport')
b.n(r'At a temperature where thermal voltage VT=0.025 V, electron mobility is 1200 cm²/(V·s). Find the diffusion coefficient in cm²/s.',30,'VT=0.025 V; μn=1200 cm²/(V·s); nondegenerate carriers','Find Dn.','Einstein relation',r'D_n/\mu_n=V_T',r'In a nondegenerate semiconductor, diffusion and mobility obey the Einstein relation. | Multiply mobility by thermal voltage: 1200×0.025. | Dn=30 cm²/s.','The thermal voltage multiplies mobility; it is not a dimensionless temperature.',topic='carrier-transport',unit='cm²/s')
b.n(r'An ideal diode has saturation current 1 nA, ideality factor 1 and VT=25 mV. At forward voltage V=VT ln(1001), find the current in μA.',1,'IS=1 nA; η=1; exponential argument ln(1001)','Find exact diode current including the −1 term.','Shockley diode law',r'I=I_S(e^{V/V_T}-1)',r'At the specified voltage, the exponential is exactly 1001. | Subtract 1 to obtain the multiplier 1000. | I=1000 nA=1 μA.','Keeping the −1 term matters when an exact exponential value is supplied.',topic='pn-junctions',unit='μA')
b.m(r'An NPN BJT in forward-active operation has common-base current gain α=0.98. Its common-emitter current gain β is:','C',['0.98','1.02','49','98'],'Forward-active transistor; α=IC/IE','Convert α to β=IC/IB.','BJT current-gain definitions',r'\beta=\alpha/(1-\alpha)',r'Emitter current is IC+IB, so α=β/(β+1). | Rearranging gives β=α/(1−α). | At α=0.98, β=0.98/0.02=49, option C.','β and α have different denominators; β is not 1/α.',topic='bjt')
b.n(r'A forward-active BJT carries IC=1 mA at VT=25 mV. Find its small-signal transconductance in mS.',40,'Collector current 1 mA; thermal voltage 25 mV','Find gm.','BJT exponential linearization',r'g_m=I_C/V_T',r'The derivative of IC with respect to VBE in the exponential model is IC/VT. | Divide 0.001 A by 0.025 V to get 0.04 S. | Multiplying by 1000 gives gm=40 mS.','Use the collector current, not base current, in this expression.',topic='bjt',unit='mS',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'An ideal long-channel NMOS has β=1 mA/V², VTH=1 V, VGS=3 V and VDS=3 V. With ID=β(VGS−VTH)²/2 in saturation and no channel-length modulation, find ID in mA.',2,'Long-channel square-law model; β=1 mA/V²; overdrive 2 V; VDS=3 V','Find current after checking the region.','MOSFET region and square law',r'V_{DS}\ge V_{GS}-V_{TH},\quad I_D=\frac\beta2(V_{GS}-V_{TH})^2',r'The overdrive is 3−1=2 V. Since VDS=3≥2 V, the assumed saturation model applies. | Substitute ID=(1/2)×(2)² in mA. | The drain current is 2 mA.','The factor 1/2 depends on the β definition; the problem explicitly specifies it.',topic='mosfet',unit='mA')
b.s(r'Which statements about ideal semiconductor/optoelectronic operation are correct?',['A','C','D'],['Direct band gaps permit radiative interband transitions without phonon momentum assistance','An equilibrium PN junction has a nonzero net terminal current at zero external bias','A photodiode can convert incident photons into collected electron–hole pairs','A reverse-biased junction can have nonzero depletion capacitance'],'Equilibrium junctions; basic optoelectronic mechanisms','Select all physically valid statements.','Band structure, equilibrium and charge storage',r'C_j=\frac{dQ}{dV}',r'A direct transition can conserve crystal momentum without requiring a phonon, supporting radiative recombination. | At equilibrium drift and diffusion currents balance, so the net external current is zero. | Photogeneration can be collected, and depletion charge varies with voltage, giving junction capacitance. Select A, C and D.','Zero DC junction current does not imply zero differential capacitance.',topic='optoelectronic-devices')
b.n(r'For a one-sided abrupt p⁺n junction, use εs=10⁻¹² F/cm, ND=10¹⁶ cm⁻³, q=1.6×10⁻¹⁹ C and total depletion potential ψ=0.8 V. Find W≈sqrt(2εsψ/(qND)) in μm.',0.31622776601683794,'p-side much more heavily doped; stated depletion approximation; total potential 0.8 V','Find the depletion width in micrometers.','One-sided depletion approximation',r'W\simeq\sqrt{\frac{2\epsilon_s\psi}{qN_D}}',r'The ratio inside the square root is (2×10⁻¹²×0.8)/(1.6×10⁻¹⁹×10¹⁶)=10⁻⁹ cm². | The square root is 3.16227766×10⁻⁵ cm. | Since 1 cm=10⁴ μm, W≈0.316227766 μm.','ψ is the total junction potential; do not add another built-in voltage to it.',topic='pn-junctions',unit='μm',difficulty='hard')
b.n(r'An electron concentration gradient is dn/dx=−10¹⁸ cm⁻⁴. With Dn=25 cm²/s and q=1.6×10⁻¹⁹ C, find the electron diffusion current density in A/cm².',-4,'Dn=25 cm²/s; concentration decreases with x','Find signed electron diffusion current density.','Diffusion current sign',r'J_{n,diff}=qD_n\frac{dn}{dx}',r'Electron particle flux is −Dn dn/dx, but electron charge is −q. | Their product gives conventional current qDn dn/dx. | Substitution gives 1.6×10⁻¹⁹×25×(−10¹⁸)=−4 A/cm².','Electron particle-flow direction and conventional electron-current direction are opposite.',topic='carrier-transport',unit='A/cm²',difficulty='hard')
b.n(r'A photodiode has quantum efficiency 0.8 and receives 1 mW of photons each of energy 2 eV. Using q=1.6×10⁻¹⁹ C and 1 eV=1.6×10⁻¹⁹ J, find the photocurrent magnitude in mA.',0.4,'η=0.8; incident power 10⁻³ W; photon energy 3.2×10⁻¹⁹ J','Find collected photon-generated current.','Quantum efficiency',r'I_{ph}=\eta qP/(h\nu)',r'The photon arrival rate is 10⁻³/(3.2×10⁻¹⁹)=3.125×10¹⁵ s⁻¹. | Multiplying by efficiency 0.8 gives 2.5×10¹⁵ collected electrons/s. | Multiplying by q gives 4×10⁻⁴ A=0.4 mA.','Quantum efficiency multiplies the photon rate, not the photon energy.',topic='optoelectronic-devices',unit='mA')
b.s(r'For an ideal MOS capacitor with a p-type substrate, which statements hold as gate voltage is varied?',['A','C'],['A sufficiently negative gate attracts holes into accumulation','A positive gate immediately makes the substrate more p-type','A sufficiently positive gate can form an electron inversion layer','The oxide must conduct DC current for a static field to exist'],'Ideal insulating oxide; p-type body; conventional gate bias reference','Identify accumulation and inversion mechanisms.','MOS electrostatics',r'Q_G+Q_S=0\quad\text{for ideal planar charge balance}',r'A negative gate is balanced by positive semiconductor charge, attracting majority holes into accumulation. | A positive gate repels holes, then can attract a minority-electron inversion layer at sufficiently strong bias. | The oxide sustains an electric field without steady conduction. Select A and C.','A capacitor supports static electric fields without a DC conduction path.',topic='mos-capacitor')

b=Bank('subject-04','analog','operational-amplifiers','Analog Circuits · Amplifiers, Feedback & Waveforms','004');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'The shown ideal inverting op-amp uses Rin=10 kΩ and Rf=30 kΩ, with the noninverting terminal grounded. For Vin=1 V and ±12 V supplies, find Vout in volts.',-3,'Ideal op-amp; negative feedback; Rin=10 kΩ; Rf=30 kΩ; input 1 V; unsaturated output','Find the closed-loop output.','Virtual ground and inverting gain',r'V_o=-\frac{R_f}{R_{in}}V_i',r'Negative feedback holds the inverting node at virtual ground in linear operation. | KCL gives (1−0)/10k=(0−Vo)/30k. | Vo=−3 V, within the ±12 V supply limits, so the assumed operating region is consistent.','Virtual ground is a voltage condition, not a direct physical connection.',unit='V',diagram=('assets/diagrams/inverting-amplifier.svg','Ideal inverting amplifier with 10 kΩ input, 30 kΩ feedback, grounded noninverting input, ±12 V supplies.'))
b.n(r'An ideal inverting summer has two input resistors of 10 kΩ, feedback resistance 20 kΩ, and inputs 1 V and 2 V. Assuming linear operation, find Vout in volts.',-6,'Equal input resistors 10 kΩ; Rf=20 kΩ; two DC inputs 1 V,2 V','Find the summed output voltage.','Op-amp summing node',r'V_o=-R_f\left(\frac{V_1}{R_1}+\frac{V_2}{R_2}\right)',r'The input currents into virtual ground are 0.1 mA and 0.2 mA. | Their sum 0.3 mA must flow through the feedback resistor. | Vo=−(0.3 mA)(20 kΩ)=−6 V.','Add input currents with their resistor weights before applying the feedback resistance.',unit='V')
b.n(r'An ideal inverting integrator has RC=0.01 s, constant input 1 V from t=0, and Vout(0)=0. Find Vout at t=1 ms, in volts.',-0.1,'RC=0.01 s; constant 1 V input; zero initial output','Find the integrated output after 1 ms.','Op-amp integration',r'V_o(t)=V_o(0)-\frac1{RC}\int_0^t V_i(\tau)d\tau',r'The input integral over 1 ms is 1×0.001 V·s. | Divide by RC=0.01 s and apply the inverting sign. | Vout=−0.001/0.01=−0.1 V.','Include both the integration interval and the initial capacitor/output state.',unit='V')
b.n(r'A common-emitter small-signal amplifier has gm=40 mS and RC=2.5 kΩ. Ignore transistor output resistance and emitter degeneration, and assume an ideal AC voltage drive vbe. Find vo/vbe.',-100,'gm=0.04 S; RC=2500 Ω; collector supply is AC ground','Find midband small-signal gain.','Common-emitter transconductance gain',r'A_v=-g_mR_C',r'The collector current increment is gm vbe. | Its drop through RC reduces collector voltage, so vo=−gmRC vbe. | Gain is −0.04×2500=−100.','The gain is negative because a larger base drive pulls the collector voltage downward.',topic='amplifiers')
b.n(r'A matched BJT differential pair has an ideal tail source, gm=2 mS per transistor and RC=10 kΩ per collector. For differential input vd=vb1−vb2, find vo1/vd in differential mode, neglecting ro.',-10,'Matched pair; ideal tail; equal collector resistors; single-ended collector-1 output','Find single-ended differential gain.','Differential half-circuit',r'v_{be1}=v_d/2,\quad A_{d,1}=-g_mR_C/2',r'Odd-mode symmetry makes the shared emitter node an AC virtual ground. | Transistor 1 receives half the differential input, giving ic1=gm vd/2. | Its collector output is −RC ic1, so gain=−0.002×10000/2=−10.','Single-ended differential gain is half the collector-to-collector gain magnitude.',topic='amplifiers',difficulty='hard')
b.n(r'A negative-feedback amplifier has real open-loop gain A=1000 and feedback factor β=0.1 at low frequency. Find its exact closed-loop gain.',9.900990099009901,'A=1000; β=0.1; negative feedback','Find A/(1+Aβ).','Closed-loop feedback',r'A_f=\frac{A}{1+A\beta}',r'The error is vi−βvo and output is A times that error. | Rearranging vo=A(vi−βvo) yields vo/vi=A/(1+Aβ). | Substitution gives 1000/101≈9.900990099, slightly below 1/β=10.','The approximation 1/β is not exact at finite loop gain.',topic='feedback')
b.m(r'For a negative-feedback loop with a single relevant unity-gain crossover, the unwrapped loop phase there is −135°. What is its phase margin?','C',['−45°','0°','45°','135°'],'Unity loop-magnitude crossover; loop phase −135°','Find the angular separation from −180°.','Phase margin',r'PM=180^\circ+\angle L(j\omega_{gc})',r'At unity loop gain, the critical feedback phase is −180°. | The measured phase is −135°, which is 45° away from that point. | PM=180−135=45°, option C.','Use loop phase at gain crossover, not gain at phase crossover.',topic='feedback')
b.n(r'An equal-R equal-C Wien network uses R=10 kΩ and C=10 nF. Find its zero-phase frequency in hertz.',1591.5494309189535,'Equal Wien lead-lag components; R=10⁴ Ω; C=10⁻⁸ F','Find the oscillation-selection frequency.','Wien-network frequency',r'f_0=\frac1{2\pi RC}',r'The equal-component network has zero phase when ω0RC=1. | RC=10⁻⁴ s, hence ω0=10000 rad/s. | Divide by 2π to obtain f0≈1591.549431 Hz.','Angular frequency and cyclic frequency differ by 2π.',topic='oscillators',unit='Hz')
b.m(r'For an ideal half-wave rectifier with resistive load and input 10 sin(ωt) V, what is the average output voltage over a complete period?','B',[r'$10$ V',r'$10/\pi$ V',r'$20/\pi$ V',r'$5\sqrt2$ V'],'Ideal diode passes positive half-cycles; no smoothing capacitor','Find the DC component of the rectified waveform.','Half-wave average',r'V_{avg}=\frac1{2\pi}\int_0^\pi V_m\sin\theta\,d\theta',r'The output is 10sinθ on the positive half-cycle and zero on the negative half. | The positive-half integral is 20; dividing by the whole-period length 2π gives 10/π. | This is the average, not the peak or RMS voltage.','Use the full-period denominator even though conduction occurs for only half a period.',topic='diode-circuits')
b.s(r'For an ideal op-amp operating linearly with stable negative feedback, which statements hold?',['A','B','D'],['Input currents are zero','The two input voltages are equal in the ideal infinite-gain limit','Output voltage must always be zero','Input differential voltage is approximately zero for sufficiently large finite open-loop gain'],'Linear operation; stable negative feedback; ideal input resistance','Select valid operating constraints.','Ideal op-amp constraints',r'V_o=A(V_+-V_-)',r'Infinite input resistance makes each input current zero. | A finite unsaturated output with ideal infinite gain requires V+=V−; for large finite A the difference is Vo/A. | The output can take a nonzero value set by feedback, so C is false. Select A, B and D.','The equal-input rule cannot be applied unconditionally to saturated comparators.')
b.s(r'Which modifications generally improve the low-frequency accuracy of a voltage-feedback amplifier while it remains stable and linear?',['A','C'],['Increase loop gain magnitude without changing its sign or compromising stability','Remove negative feedback altogether','Increase the amplifier’s actual input resistance to reduce loading of a finite-resistance source','Force the output into a supply rail'],'Stable linear operation; accuracy refers to feedback-set gain and source loading','Choose valid gain/impedance design directions.','Feedback error and loading',r'A_f=\frac{1/\beta}{1+1/(A\beta)}',r'The fractional deviation from 1/β decreases as a properly phased stable loop gain grows. | A higher input resistance draws less current from a finite-resistance signal source. | Removing feedback or saturating the output destroys these conditions. Select A and C.','Increasing loop gain is useful only while the stability and linear-operation assumptions remain valid.',topic='feedback')
b.n(r'A dominant-pole amplifier has unity-gain frequency 1 MHz and open-loop transfer approximately A0/(1+s/ωp). Well above its dominant pole, estimate its closed-loop bandwidth for noninverting noise gain 20, in kHz.',50,'One dominant pole; unity-gain frequency 1000 kHz; noise gain 20','Estimate closed-loop −3 dB bandwidth under the gain-bandwidth approximation.','Dominant-pole gain-bandwidth product',r'NG\,f_{BW}\simeq f_T',r'For a dominant-pole response, the gain-bandwidth product is approximately constant. | Use the noninverting noise gain of 20 for the loop crossover estimate. | fBW≈1000/20=50 kHz. The approximation requires separation from the dominant pole and higher poles.','Use noise gain rather than an inverting signal-gain magnitude when the two differ.',topic='feedback',unit='kHz',difficulty='hard')

b=Bank('subject-06','control','transfer-functions','Control Systems · Stability, Error & State Models','004');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'With unity negative feedback and forward transfer G(s)=10/(s+2), find the closed-loop pole.',-12,'Unity negative feedback; G(s)=10/(s+2)','Find the pole of G/(1+G).','Closed-loop characteristic equation',r'T(s)=\frac{G(s)}{1+G(s)}',r'The denominator becomes s+2+10. | Thus T(s)=10/(s+12). | Its only pole is −12 s⁻¹.','Negative feedback contributes a plus sign to the characteristic denominator.',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'The characteristic polynomial is s³+2s²+3s+K. For positive K, find the upper stability-boundary value of K (not included in the strictly stable interval).',6,'Real cubic; K>0; strict asymptotic stability','Find the boundary where the Routh first-column entry vanishes.','Routh–Hurwitz cubic criterion',r'K>0,\quad2\cdot3>K',r'The Routh first column is 1,2,(6−K)/2,K. | Strict positivity requires 0<K<6. | The upper boundary is K=6; equality is not strictly stable.','The question asks for the boundary value, while the stable interval excludes it.',topic='stability',difficulty='hard')
b.n(r'A stable unity-feedback type-1 system has velocity error constant Kv=10 s⁻¹. Find its steady-state error for a unit ramp.',0.1,'Stable loop; type 1; Kv=10; ramp slope 1','Find unit-ramp steady-state error.','Static error constants',r'e_{ss,ramp}=1/K_v',r'A unit ramp has transform 1/s². | Applying the final-value theorem to the error gives the reciprocal velocity constant. | ess=1/10=0.1.','Type 1 has zero step error, but generally nonzero ramp error.',topic='time-response')
b.m(r'The magnitude asymptote of H(s)=1/(1+s/10)² at frequencies well above 10 rad/s has slope:','D',['+40 dB/decade','+20 dB/decade','−20 dB/decade','−40 dB/decade'],'Two coincident real poles; no finite zeros','Find high-frequency Bode magnitude slope.','Bode pole contributions',r'20\log_{10}|H(j\omega)|\simeq-40\log_{10}(\omega/10)',r'Each first-order pole contributes −20 dB/decade above its corner. | There are two such poles. | Adding their contributions gives −40 dB/decade, option D.','Pole multiplicity changes the asymptotic slope.',topic='frequency-response')
b.n(r'For a unity-feedback root locus of G(s)=K/[s(s+2)] with K≥0, find the real-axis asymptote centroid.',-1,'Two open-loop poles at 0,−2; no finite zeros','Find the root-locus asymptote centroid.','Root-locus asymptotes',r'\sigma_a=\frac{\sum p_i-\sum z_i}{n-m}',r'The sum of the poles is −2 and there are no zero contributions. | The number of asymptotes is n−m=2. | Their common centroid is −2/2=−1.','The centroid uses all poles and zeros, including multiplicities.',topic='root-locus')
b.n(r'For the state equation xdot=diag(−1,−2)x with x(0)=(1,1)ᵀ, find x2(ln2).',0.25,'Diagonal state matrix; initial second state 1','Find second-state value at ln2.','State transition matrix',r'x(t)=e^{At}x(0)',r'The second state obeys dx2/dt=−2x2 independently. | Its solution is x2=e⁻²ᵗ. | At t=ln2 this is e⁻²ˡⁿ²=1/4.','A diagonal A decouples the states; use the eigenvalue associated with the second component.',topic='state-space-analysis')
b.s(r'For the state model A=[[0,1],[−2,−3]], B=[0,1]ᵀ and C=[1,0], which statements hold?',['A','B','D'],['The controllability matrix has rank 2','The observability matrix has rank 2','A has an eigenvalue at +1','The unforced state dynamics are asymptotically stable'],'Two-state LTI realization; D=0','Assess controllability, observability and stability.','State realization tests',r'\mathcal C=[B\ AB],\quad\mathcal O=\begin{bmatrix}C\\CA\end{bmatrix}',r'AB=(1,−3)ᵀ, so det[B AB]=−1 and controllability rank is 2. | C and CA form the identity matrix, so observability rank is also 2. | The characteristic polynomial is s²+3s+2 with roots −1,−2. Thus D holds and C does not. Select A, B and D.','Full controllability does not by itself imply stability; the poles must also be checked.',topic='state-space-analysis',difficulty='hard')
b.n(r'A loop has magnitude |L(jωpc)|=0.25 at its relevant −180° phase crossover. Find its gain margin as a multiplicative factor.',4,'Loop magnitude 0.25 at phase crossover','Find the factor that brings magnitude to 1.','Gain margin',r'GM=1/|L(j\omega_{pc})|',r'At phase −180°, multiplying loop gain by GM brings the Nyquist point to −1. | Required factor is 1/0.25. | The gain margin factor is 4, equivalent to about 12.04 dB.','The factor and its decibel representation are different requested outputs.',topic='frequency-response')
b.m(r'Which controller term produces an unbounded DC gain as s→0 and can eliminate constant-reference steady-state error in a stable suitable feedback loop?','A',['Integral term Ki/s','Derivative term Kd s alone','A finite proportional term alone','A pure time delay alone'],'Constant reference; closed-loop stability and regulation feasibility assumed','Identify the internal-model term for a constant.','Integral control',r'C_I(s)=K_I/s',r'The integral term introduces a pole at the origin and arbitrarily large low-frequency gain. | In a stable loop with the required regulation path it accumulates persistent constant error until that error vanishes. | Derivative gain instead vanishes at DC, and finite proportional gain need not remove the error. Select A.','Integral action is not a guarantee of stability or of avoiding actuator saturation.',topic='compensators')
b.n(r'A first-order system has transfer 5/(2s+1). Find its unit-step steady-state output.',5,'Stable transfer function; unit step; zero initial state','Find the final response value.','Final-value theorem',r'y(\infty)=\lim_{s\to0}s\,H(s)/s',r'The transfer pole is −1/2, so the relevant final-value condition is satisfied. | The unit step makes the final value equal the DC gain H(0). | H(0)=5.','Check stability before applying the final-value theorem.',topic='time-response',difficulty='easy')
b.s(r'Which statements about standard negative-feedback control are correct?',['A','C'],['The sensitivity to forward-path gain is reduced when a stable loop has large low-frequency loop gain','Negative feedback guarantees stability for every plant','A right-half-plane internal mode makes the closed-loop state dynamics unstable even if hidden by a measured-transfer cancellation','A phase margin alone gives the exact nonlinear saturation response'],'Ordinary finite-dimensional LTI closed-loop dynamics; no restriction to one hidden transfer path','Select valid robustness/stability statements.','Feedback and internal stability',r'S(s)=\frac1{1+L(s)}',r'A stable large-loop-gain region makes sensitivity 1/(1+L) small. | Loop phase and dynamics can cause negative feedback to destabilize a plant, so B is false. | A right-half-plane internal mode grows; hiding it by a particular transfer cancellation does not restore internal stability. Linear phase margin does not specify saturation behavior. Select A and C.','Input-output cancellation can hide an unstable internal state.',topic='stability')
b.n(r'For a canonical second-order system with ωn=5 rad/s and ζ=0.6, find its damped oscillation frequency in rad/s.',4,'Natural frequency 5; damping ratio 0.6<1','Find ωd.','Underdamped poles',r'\omega_d=\omega_n\sqrt{1-\zeta^2}',r'The imaginary part of the conjugate poles is ωn√(1−ζ²). | Here 1−0.36=0.64 and its square root is 0.8. | Therefore ωd=5×0.8=4 rad/s.','The oscillation frequency is smaller than the undamped natural frequency.',topic='time-response',unit='rad/s')

b=Bank('subject-07','communications','information-theory','Communications · Information, Modulation & Detection','004');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'A memoryless source has probabilities (1/2,1/4,1/4). Find its entropy in bits per symbol.',1.5,'Three-symbol probability mass function','Find Shannon entropy using log base 2.','Source entropy',r'H=-\sum_i p_i\log_2p_i',r'The probability-1/2 symbol contributes (1/2)×1=0.5 bits. | Each probability-1/4 symbol contributes (1/4)×2=0.5 bits. | Total entropy is 0.5+0.5+0.5=1.5 bits/symbol.','A three-symbol alphabet need not have entropy log₂3 unless all symbols are equally likely.',unit='bits/symbol')
b.n(r'An ideal bandlimited AWGN channel has B=1 kHz and received signal-to-noise power ratio 15 (linear). Find Shannon capacity in kbit/s.',4,'B=1000 Hz; S/N=15 linear; AWGN capacity assumptions','Find capacity.','Gaussian channel capacity',r'C=B\log_2(1+S/N)',r'The logarithm argument is 1+15=16. | log₂16=4 bits per second per hertz. | Multiply by 1000 Hz to get 4000 bit/s=4 kbit/s.','Use the linear SNR inside the formula; 15 is not a decibel value.',unit='kbit/s')
b.n(r'For conventional single-tone AM with modulation index m=0.5 and carrier power Pc=100 W, find total transmitted power in watts.',112.5,'Conventional full-carrier AM; m=0.5; Pc=100 W','Find carrier-plus-sideband power.','AM power distribution',r'P_T=P_c(1+m^2/2)',r'The sideband amplitudes are each m/2 times the carrier amplitude. | Their total power is Pc m²/2=100×0.25/2=12.5 W. | Adding the 100 W carrier gives 112.5 W.','Sideband power depends on the square of modulation index.',topic='analog-communication',unit='W')
b.n(r'An FM signal has peak frequency deviation 5 kHz and highest message frequency 1 kHz. Using Carson’s approximation, find its bandwidth in kHz.',12,'Δf=5 kHz; highest modulating frequency fm=1 kHz','Estimate FM occupied bandwidth by Carson’s rule.','Carson bandwidth estimate',r'B\simeq2(\Delta f+f_m)',r'Add peak deviation and maximum modulating frequency: 5+1=6 kHz. | Carson’s approximate total bandwidth is twice this sum. | The estimate is 12 kHz; it is not a strict finite spectral support.','FM has infinitely many theoretical sidebands; Carson’s rule is an engineering approximation.',topic='analog-communication',unit='kHz')
b.n(r'Uniform PCM uses 8 bits/sample at 8 ksample/s with no overhead. Find its raw bit rate in kbit/s.',64,'8 quantization bits per sample; 8000 samples per second','Find uncoded serial bit rate.','PCM rate',r'R_b=n_bf_s',r'Each sample contributes 8 bits. | Multiply 8 bits/sample by 8000 samples/s. | The stream rate is 64000 bit/s=64 kbit/s.','Framing/coding overhead is excluded by the stated assumption.',topic='digital-communication',unit='kbit/s',difficulty='easy')
b.m(r'For an ideal coherent BPSK link in AWGN with equiprobable bits and correct carrier synchronization, the BER is:','B',[r'$Q(\sqrt{E_b/N_0})$',r'$Q(\sqrt{2E_b/N_0})$',r'$Q(E_b/N_0)$',r'$e^{-2E_b/N_0}$'],'Coherent antipodal signaling; AWGN two-sided PSD N0/2','Choose the BER law.','Antipodal Gaussian detection',r'P_b=Q\left(\sqrt{2E_b/N_0}\right)',r'The matched-filter decision variable has means ±√Eb and noise variance N0/2 in a unit-energy basis. | Normalizing the distance from either mean to the zero threshold gives √Eb/√(N0/2). | This is √(2Eb/N0), so the Gaussian-tail error probability is option B.','The factor 2 follows from the two-sided noise PSD convention.',topic='probability-of-error')
b.n(r'A 16-QAM constellation carries equiprobable symbols at 1000 symbols/s. With no coding overhead, find the bit rate in bit/s.',4000,'16 symbol choices; 1000 symbols/s','Find log₂(M) times symbol rate.','Bits per symbol',r'R_b=R_s\log_2M',r'16 distinct choices encode log₂16=4 bits per symbol. | At 1000 symbols/s the data rate is 4×1000. | The uncoded rate is 4000 bit/s.','Bits per second and symbols per second differ when a symbol represents several bits.',topic='digital-communication',unit='bit/s',difficulty='easy')
b.s(r'Which statements hold for AWGN detection with known deterministic signals and the usual matched-filter model?',['A','C','D'],['A matched filter maximizes output SNR at its design sampling instant','Maximum-likelihood and MAP rules always coincide for unequal prior probabilities','For equal priors and equal decision costs, MAP reduces to maximum likelihood','Matched-filter impulse response is proportional to a time-reversed conjugated signal template'],'Known finite-energy signals; white Gaussian noise; stated prior/cost conditions','Select valid receiver principles.','Matched filtering and Bayesian decisions',r'h(t)=k\,s^*(T-t)',r'Cauchy–Schwarz bounds the output SNR, with equality for the reversed conjugate template. | MAP maximizes likelihood times prior (for equal costs); unequal priors generally shift its decision rule. | With equal priors and costs, maximizing the posterior reduces to maximizing likelihood. Select A, C and D.','The equivalence of ML and MAP requires the relevant prior/cost assumptions.',topic='digital-communication',difficulty='hard')
b.n(r'A binary linear code has minimum Hamming distance dmin=5. Find the largest number of arbitrary bit errors it can always correct per codeword under nearest-codeword decoding.',2,'Binary code with minimum distance 5','Find guaranteed correction radius.','Hamming-distance correction bound',r't=\left\lfloor\frac{d_{min}-1}{2}\right\rfloor',r'Correctable error balls must not overlap between codewords. | Unique decoding of t errors requires 2t<dmin. | With dmin=5, the largest integer satisfying this is t=2.','Detection capability dmin−1 differs from correction capability.',topic='error-control-coding')
b.n(r'A superheterodyne receiver uses high-side injection: fLO=fRF+fIF. For fRF=100 MHz and fIF=10 MHz, find the image frequency in MHz.',120,'High-side LO; desired RF 100 MHz; IF 10 MHz','Find the other positive RF that mixes to the same IF.','Superheterodyne image',r'f_{image}=f_{LO}+f_{IF}=f_{RF}+2f_{IF}',r'The LO is 100+10=110 MHz. | Both RF=LO−IF and RF=LO+IF give the same difference-frequency magnitude. | The undesired image is 110+10=120 MHz.','For high-side injection the image is above the desired RF by twice the IF.',topic='analog-communication',unit='MHz')
b.s(r'For a wide-sense stationary real process with finite second moment, which statements are correct?',['A','B','D'],['Autocorrelation is even','The power spectral density is nonnegative','Autocorrelation at zero must be zero','Autocorrelation at zero equals mean-square value'],'Real WSS process; finite average power','Select valid autocorrelation and spectral properties.','Wiener–Khinchin properties',r'R_X(0)=E[X^2],\quad S_X(f)=\mathcal F\{R_X(\tau)\}',r'Commutativity of real scalar multiplication and stationarity give R(τ)=R(−τ). | Zero lag gives E[X²], which need not vanish. | The PSD is nonnegative because it describes spectral power. Select A, B and D.','Autocorrelation includes mean-square power, whereas autocovariance subtracts the mean contribution.',topic='random-processes')
b.n(r'A binary symmetric channel flips bits independently with probability p=0.25. Find its capacity in bits per channel use.',0.18872187554086717,'Memoryless BSC; crossover probability 1/4','Find maximum mutual information per use.','BSC capacity',r'C=1-H_2(p)',r'The binary entropy is −0.25log₂0.25−0.75log₂0.75≈0.8112781245. | Symmetric input probability 1/2 maximizes output entropy to 1 bit. | Capacity is 1−0.8112781245≈0.1887218755 bits/use.','A raw bit per use does not mean one reliably transferable information bit per use.',unit='bits/use',difficulty='hard')

b=Bank('subject-08','electromagnetics','transmission-lines','Electromagnetics · Lines, Waves & Fields','004');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'A lossless line has Z0=50 Ω and a purely resistive load ZL=100 Ω. Find the voltage reflection coefficient at the load.',0.3333333333333333,'Z0=50 Ω; ZL=100 Ω','Find ΓL.','Load reflection',r'\Gamma_L=\frac{Z_L-Z_0}{Z_L+Z_0}',r'The mismatch numerator is 100−50=50 Ω. | The denominator is 100+50=150 Ω. | ΓL=50/150=1/3, real and positive.','Voltage reflection coefficient is not the reflected power fraction.')
b.n(r'On a lossless line, the magnitude of voltage reflection coefficient is 0.5. Find VSWR.',3,'|Γ|=0.5; lossless standing wave','Find the maximum/minimum voltage ratio.','Standing-wave ratio',r'S=\frac{1+|\Gamma|}{1-|\Gamma|}',r'The voltage envelopes scale as 1+|Γ| and 1−|Γ|. | Substitute 0.5 into their ratio. | VSWR=1.5/0.5=3.','Use the reflection magnitude, not its signed real part.',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'A lossless quarter-wave transformer matches a 100 Ω load to a 25 Ω source line at its design frequency. Find the transformer characteristic impedance in ohms.',50,'Real source-line impedance 25 Ω; load 100 Ω; quarter wavelength','Find the matching section impedance.','Quarter-wave impedance transformation',r'Z_{in}=Z_t^2/Z_L,\quad Z_t=\sqrt{Z_0Z_L}',r'A quarter-wave section transforms the resistive load to Zt²/ZL. | Set the transformed impedance to 25 Ω. | Zt=√(25×100)=50 Ω.','The geometric mean, not arithmetic mean, is required.',unit='Ω')
b.n(r'A uniform lossless nonmagnetic medium has relative permittivity 4. Using c=3×10⁸ m/s, find the plane-wave phase velocity in units of 10⁸ m/s.',1.5,'μr=1; εr=4; c=3×10⁸ m/s','Find velocity divided by 10⁸ m/s.','Wave speed in a dielectric',r'v_p=\frac{c}{\sqrt{\mu_r\epsilon_r}}',r'The refractive-index factor is √4=2. | The phase velocity is 3×10⁸/2=1.5×10⁸ m/s. | In the requested units the numerical answer is 1.5.','The question requests scaled units; do not enter the full SI value.',topic='wave-propagation',unit='×10⁸ m/s',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'A uniform plane wave in a lossless medium has E-field peak magnitude 10 V/m and real intrinsic impedance 100 Ω. Find average power density in W/m².',0.5,'Peak E=10 V/m; intrinsic impedance η=100 Ω','Find time-average Poynting magnitude.','Plane-wave power',r'\langle S\rangle=E_{peak}^2/(2\eta)',r'For a traveling wave Hpeak=Epeak/η=0.1 A/m. | Sinusoidal field products average to one half of the peak product. | Average power density is 10×0.1/2=0.5 W/m².','The factor 1/2 is required for peak phasors; RMS phasors use a different convention.',topic='maxwell-equations',unit='W/m²')
b.m(r'A plane wave propagates in +z with electric field along +x in a lossless medium. Its magnetic field points along:','B',['+x','+y','−y','−z'],'E × H points in the propagation direction','Find H direction.','Poynting-vector orientation',r'\mathbf S=\mathbf E\times\mathbf H',r'The right-handed Cartesian identity is x̂×ŷ=ẑ. | Since E is along +x and power propagation is +z, H must point along +y. | Therefore option B is correct.','Reversing the cross-product order reverses the result.',topic='electromagnetic-waves')
b.n(r'For a good conductor with skin depth δ at a fixed frequency, the frequency is increased by a factor of 4 while μ and σ remain constant. Find δnew/δold.',0.5,'Good-conductor regime; unchanged permeability and conductivity; frequency quadrupled','Find the skin-depth ratio.','Skin-effect scaling',r'\delta=\sqrt{\frac{2}{\omega\mu\sigma}}',r'Skin depth varies inversely with the square root of frequency. | A fourfold frequency increase multiplies the denominator square root by 2. | Thus δnew/δold=1/2.','Skin depth scales as 1/√f, not 1/f.',topic='wave-propagation')
b.s(r'At an interface with no free surface charge and no surface current, which boundary statements hold for ordinary finite fields?',['A','B','D'],['Tangential electric field is continuous','Normal magnetic flux density is continuous','Normal electric field is always identical across different permittivities','Normal electric displacement is continuous'],'Interface without free surface sources','Select valid Maxwell boundary conditions.','Electromagnetic boundary conditions',r'\hat n\times(\mathbf E_2-\mathbf E_1)=0,\quad\hat n\cdot(\mathbf D_2-\mathbf D_1)=\rho_s',r'Faraday’s law applied to a vanishing loop gives tangential E continuity for finite fields. | Magnetic Gauss law gives continuity of normal B. Electric Gauss law gives continuity of normal D when free surface charge is zero. | Normal E can change inversely with permittivity. Select A, B and D.','Continuity of Dn does not require continuity of En across unequal permittivities.',topic='maxwell-equations',difficulty='hard')
b.n(r'An air-filled rectangular waveguide has broad dimension a=3 cm. Find the TE10 cutoff frequency in GHz, using c=3×10⁸ m/s.',5,'Air-filled rectangular guide; a=0.03 m; TE10 mode','Find the dominant-mode cutoff.','Rectangular-waveguide cutoff',r'f_{c,10}=c/(2a)',r'For TE10 the transverse index is one along the broad dimension and zero along the narrow dimension. | fc=3×10⁸/(2×0.03)=5×10⁹ Hz. | Converting to GHz gives 5.','Use the broad dimension in meters and distinguish cutoff from operating frequency.',topic='waveguides-fibers',unit='GHz')
b.n(r'A half-wave dipole has radiation resistance 73 Ω and loss resistance 7 Ω at resonance. Find its radiation efficiency in percent.',91.25,'Rrad=73 Ω; Rloss=7 Ω; same antenna terminal current','Find fraction of accepted power radiated.','Antenna radiation efficiency',r'\eta_{rad}=\frac{R_{rad}}{R_{rad}+R_{loss}}',r'Radiated and dissipated powers are proportional to the corresponding resistances for the same current. | The accepted-power resistance sum is 73+7=80 Ω. | Efficiency is 100×73/80=91.25%.','Radiation resistance represents radiated power, not heat loss.',topic='antennas')
b.s(r'For a passive lossless two-port with real equal reference impedances, which S-parameter statements necessarily hold?',['A','C'],['Its S matrix is unitary','It must have S11=0 at every frequency','For excitation at port 1 alone, |S11|²+|S21|²=1','It must amplify available power above unity'],'Passive lossless network; normalized power waves; matched termination at unexcited port','Select energy-conservation constraints.','Scattering power conservation',r'S^\dagger S=I',r'Losslessness preserves the squared norm of incident and outgoing power waves. | Setting the port-2 incident wave to zero makes reflected plus transmitted power fractions sum to 1. | A lossless network may reflect, so matching is not automatic, and passive available power gain cannot exceed 1. Select A and C.','Lossless and matched are different properties.',topic='transmission-lines',difficulty='hard')
b.n(r'At normal incidence from medium 1 with real impedance η1=100 Ω to medium 2 with η2=200 Ω, find the electric-field transmission coefficient Et/Ei at the boundary.',1.3333333333333333,'Lossless media; normal incidence; electric-amplitude ratio at interface','Find electric-field transmission coefficient.','Plane-wave interface matching',r'\tau_E=1+\Gamma_E=\frac{2\eta_2}{\eta_1+\eta_2}',r'The electric reflection coefficient is (200−100)/(200+100)=1/3. | Tangential E continuity gives transmitted field equal to incident plus reflected at the boundary. | Therefore Et/Ei=1+1/3=4/3. This amplitude above 1 does not imply power gain.','Power transmission also includes the impedance ratio; it is not simply |τE|².',topic='electromagnetic-waves')
save_batch(banks,'batch-004')
